<header class="main-header">
  <div class="series-title">Likutay Halachos · Choshen Mishpat</div>
  <h1>One Who Appoints an Agent to Collect His Debt 2 — Part 2</h1>
  <p style="text-align:center; font-size:1.1em; color:#555;">הלכות העושה שליח לגבות חובו, הלכה ב</p>
  <div class="subtitle">One Who Appoints an Agent to Collect His Debt — Halachah 2 (§§13–23) · Based on "BaChatzotzerot" — Likutay Moharan I:5 · Milah and pri'ah, the Shaim, Shabbos, the future Geulah, halvaah, and simchas hamitzvah</div>
</header>

<div class="section-header">§13</div>
<p>And this is the aspect of <span class="key-term">milah</span> and <span class="key-term">pri'ah</span> [the two stages of circumcision] — and "one who circumcised but did not perform <span class="key-term">pri'ah</span>, it is as though he did not circumcise," as our Sages, <em>zichronam livrachah</em>, said. For the skin of the <span class="key-term">orlah</span> and the skin of the <span class="key-term">pri'ah</span> that cover the holy <span class="key-term">Bris</span> are the aspect of two <span class="key-term">k'lipos</span> [shells] that cover the <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span>, which every person must toil to cut, break, and nullify.</p>
<p>For the skin of the <span class="key-term">orlah</span> is the aspect of complete evil, as is known — this is the aspect of the complete <span class="key-term">k'lipah</span> that entices the person away from the ways of Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span> and seeks to prevent him from the path of life entirely and to stop him completely from His <span class="key-term">mitzvos</span>, <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span>, <span class="key-term">chas v'shalom</span>. And this one must certainly subdue and break.</p>
<p>But even when one merits to cut and break this <span class="key-term">k'lipah</span> and merits the service of Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span> — to perform His <span class="key-term">mitzvos</span>, <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span>, to depart from evil and to do good — nevertheless, one still must break the thin <span class="key-term">k'lipah</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">nogah</span>, which is the aspect of the skin of the <span class="key-term">pri'ah</span>. Its hold derives from one who has not merited to purify himself completely and still desires the reward of <span class="key-term">Olam Haba</span> for his <span class="key-term">mitzvos</span> — for he is still a <span class="key-term">m'kabeil</span>, from which comes the hold of sadness and <span class="key-term">din</span>, from which the hold of the <span class="key-term">k'lipos</span> derives, as above. But because this <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span> is not a complete <span class="key-term">m'kabeil</span> — for he has, after all, merited to earn a full reward in return for his <span class="key-term">mitzvos</span> — it is only that there is in him a thin hold of the aspect of <span class="key-term">m'kabeil</span>, the aspect of sadness. And therefore through this there derives a thin <span class="key-term">k'lipah</span>, which is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">k'lipah</span> of <span class="key-term">pri'ah</span>, which one must tear and banish in order to reveal the illumination of <span class="key-term">Tzadik Y'sod Olam</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">mashpia</span>. For it is impossible for the illumination of the <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">mashpia</span>, to be revealed except when one purifies himself until there is no hold of <span class="key-term">m'kabeil</span> in him at all — that is, when he nullifies both the aforementioned <span class="key-term">k'lipos</span>, nullifying also the hold of the thin, second <span class="key-term">k'lipah</span> — that is, when he does not desire to receive even the reward of <span class="key-term">Olam Haba</span> for his <span class="key-term">mitzvos</span> etc., as above. Through this specifically the illumination of the <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">mashpia</span>, is revealed, as above.</p>
<p>And therefore it is called <span class="key-term">pri'ah</span> — a term of payment and discharge [the same root as <span class="key-term">pri'as ba'al chov</span>, the payment of a debtor]. For through all the <span class="key-term">mitzvos</span> that are performed, one pays and discharges to Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span> the loan-debt that is owed to Him from the beginning of creation, as above at the start of the discussion — see above. But it is impossible to pay and discharge the debt to Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span> except through the <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span> who performs the <span class="key-term">mitzvah</span> with <span class="key-term">simchah</span> from the <span class="key-term">mitzvah</span> itself — through which specifically the entire world ascends and returns to its root etc., as above. It follows that specifically through the aspect of the <span class="key-term">mitzvah</span> of <span class="key-term">pri'ah</span> — which is the aspect of the aforementioned <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span> who subdues the second <span class="key-term">k'lipah</span>, as above, by not desiring to receive any <span class="key-term">Olam Haba</span> — through this specifically one pays and discharges to Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span> the entire debt of the creation, as above. And therefore it is called <span class="key-term">pri'ah</span> — the aspect of <span class="key-term">pri'as ba'al chov</span> [the payment of the debtor] etc., as above.</p>

<div class="section-header">§14</div>
<p>And this is the aspect of the cup of wine that is brought for the blessing after the <span class="key-term">milah</span> — this is the aspect of the wine that gladdens, which one merits through the <span class="key-term">mitzvah</span> of <span class="key-term">milah</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">simchah</span>, as above. For the <span class="key-term">simchah</span> of the future is designated by the name of wine that gladdens, the aspect of <span class="scripture">"the wine that is preserved for the future"</span> — as it is written: <span class="scripture">"We will rejoice and be glad in You; we will recall Your love more than wine"</span> <span class="source">(Shir HaShirim 1:4)</span> — as above.</p>

<div class="section-header">§15</div>
<p>And this is the aspect of the name [<span class="key-term">shaim</span>] that is given to the infant after the <span class="key-term">milah</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">simchah</span>, as above. For the person was essentially created in order that he should merit, through his good deeds, to include "after the creation" in "before the creation" — to nullify the sadness and the <span class="key-term">din</span> from the world, to nullify the aspect of: <span class="scripture">"And He was saddened to His heart"</span> <span class="source">(Genesis 6:6)</span> — which is said when the world does not conduct itself properly, <span class="key-term">chas v'shalom</span>. Rather, that there should be fulfilled: <span class="scripture">"Hashem shall rejoice in His works; Israel shall rejoice in its Maker"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 104:31, 149:2)</span>.</p>
<p>For it is impossible that "after the creation" should be included in "before the creation" — that is, that the world should return to its root — except through the true <span class="key-term">simchah</span> from the <span class="key-term">mitzvah</span> itself, which is His unity. For <span class="scripture">"there is no sadness before the Place"</span> [i.e. before Hashem]. And therefore as long as the world is the aspect of a <span class="key-term">m'kabeil</span> and there is in it some hold of the aspect of sadness, it is unable to be included in Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span>, <span class="key-term">kiv'yachol</span> — for <span class="scripture">"one may not come to the gate of the king in sackcloth"</span> <span class="source">(Esther 4:2)</span>, for there is no sadness at all there — only <span class="scripture">"strength and joy are in His place"</span> <span class="source">(I Chronicles 16:27)</span>. And as long as the world has a hold of the aspect of sadness, it is impossible to be included there in its root — for there is only <span class="key-term">simchah</span> and joy, and there is no sadness before the Place, as above.</p>
<p>And the totality of the world is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">Shaim</span> [Name] of Hashem — for all the worlds are the aspect of the revelation of His kingship and Godliness, <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span>. For Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span> is known only from the side of His actions — through which alone we know of Him, <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span>. And this is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">Shaim</span> of Hashem, which is the aspect of the revelation of His kingship and Godliness, <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span>, as all this is known. And when the world and all that fills it is included in Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span> through the <span class="key-term">simchah shel mitzvah</span>, as above, then there is fulfilled: <span class="scripture">"Hashem is One and His Name is One"</span> — as it will be in the future, as it is written: <span class="scripture">"On that day Hashem shall be One and His Name One"</span> <span class="source">(Zechariah 14:9)</span>. That is, the <span class="key-term">Shaim</span> is included in Him, <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span> — "after the creation" is included in "before the creation" — and everything is through the aforementioned <span class="key-term">simchah</span>, as above.</p>
<p>And this is what our Rebbe, <em>zichrono livrachah</em>, wrote in another place <span class="source">(Sichos HaRa"N)</span>: that through sadness, a person does not know his name, and through this a person forgets his name after his death — and it is all through the sadness. For a person must remember his name after his death. For the person was created for this: to include <span class="key-term">zachor</span> [remember] and <span class="key-term">shamor</span> [guard] together — the <span class="key-term">aspaklaria she'ainah m'irah</span> in the <span class="key-term">aspaklaria ham'irah</span> — which is the aspect of "Hashem is One and His Name One." That is, that the entire world — which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Shaim</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">aspaklaria she'ainah m'irah</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">shamor</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">m'kabeil</span> in which the sadness takes hold — should be included, through one's good deeds with <span class="key-term">simchah</span> from the <span class="key-term">mitzvah</span> itself, until all the aspects of <span class="key-term">m'kabeil</span> and sadness are nullified from the world. Until the light of the moon shall be as the light of the sun, that there shall be fulfilled the verse: <span class="scripture">"the two great luminaries"</span> <span class="source">(Genesis 1:16)</span> — that "after the creation" shall be included in "before the creation."</p>
<p>For the true <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span>, through his good deeds, merits until all the worlds are not called <span class="key-term">m'kabeilim</span> [receivers] at all — for everything was created for his sake, and everything is included in him, who is a <span class="key-term">mashpia</span>, the aspect of "the <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span> is gracious and gives," as above. And then the <span class="key-term">aspaklaria ham'irah</span> is clarified and sweetened — for "after the creation" is included in "before the creation." And this is the aspect of remembering the name after death — for the name must be included in the aspect of <span class="key-term">zachor</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">mashpia</span>, <span class="aramaic">sitra did'chura</span> [the masculine side]. And this is through remembering one's name — that the <span class="key-term">Shaim</span>, the aspect of the totality of the worlds, is included in the aspect of <span class="key-term">zikaron</span> [remembrance], which is <span class="aramaic">sitra did'chura</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">mashpia</span>. And this one merits through the aforementioned <span class="key-term">simchah</span>, for which he was created, as above. For this is the aspect of the totality of all the worlds — which are the aspect of the <span class="key-term">Shaim</span> of Hashem — being included in their root, the aspect of "Hashem is One and His Name One," as above.</p>
<p>And therefore one who has sadness — that is, he does not merit <span class="key-term">simchah</span> and does not merit to include the <span class="key-term">Shaim</span> in its root, that is, to elevate the creation to its root, as above — therefore he forgets his name, <span class="key-term">chas v'shalom</span>. For since he has sadness, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">m'kabeil</span>, therefore the <span class="key-term">zikaron</span> is blemished — which is <span class="aramaic">sitra did'chura</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">zachor</span>, as is known. And he does not merit to include the <span class="key-term">Shaim</span> in the aspect of <span class="key-term">zikaron</span> — for he does not merit to include "after the creation" in its root, the aspect of <span class="key-term">mashpia</span>, since he does not have <span class="key-term">simchah</span>, as above.</p>
<p>And therefore after the <span class="key-term">milah</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">simchah</span>, the name is given to the infant — in order that the <span class="key-term">Shaim</span> should be included in its root, that he should merit to remember the name — that the <span class="key-term">Shaim</span>, the aspect of the totality of the worlds, should be included in its root, in the aspect of <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">mashpia</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">simchah</span>. And this is the aspect of "Hashem is One and His Name One," as above.</p>

<div class="section-header">§16</div>
<p>And this is the aspect of what our Sages, <em>zichronam livrachah</em>, said regarding <span class="key-term">Shabbos</span>: <span class="scripture">"Borrow on My account, and I will repay"</span>. For <span class="key-term">Shabbos</span> is the aspect of a <span class="key-term">mitzvah</span> with <span class="key-term">simchah</span> from the <span class="key-term">mitzvah</span> itself — whose <span class="key-term">Olam Haba</span> is in the <span class="key-term">mitzvah</span> itself. For <span class="key-term">Shabbos</span> itself is a taste of <span class="key-term">Olam Haba</span> — for <span class="key-term">Shabbos</span> is a day of <span class="key-term">simchah</span> and joy in all the worlds, and as is explained in the aforementioned teaching regarding the statement of our Sages, <em>zichronam livrachah</em>: <span class="scripture">"Remember the day of Shabbos: remember it from the first day of the week"</span> etc. — which is the aspect of a <span class="key-term">mitzvah</span> with <span class="key-term">simchah</span> from the <span class="key-term">mitzvah</span> itself. It follows that <span class="key-term">Shabbos</span> is the aspect of the aforementioned <span class="key-term">mitzvah b'simchah</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">aspaklaria ham'irah</span>. And therefore on <span class="key-term">Shabbos</span> all the worlds ascend to their root, as is known — for the essential ascent of the worlds to their root is through the <span class="key-term">simchas hamitzvah</span> from the <span class="key-term">mitzvah</span> itself, as above. Through this one pays and discharges all the debts to Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span>, as above, and then the world is taken out of the aspect of borrower and <span class="key-term">m'kabeil</span> etc., as above.</p>
<p>And this is what our Sages, <em>zichronam livrachah</em>, said about <span class="key-term">Shabbos</span>: "Borrow on My account, and I will repay." For through the holiness of <span class="key-term">Shabbos</span>, <span class="key-term">kiv'yachol</span>, Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span> becomes the aspect of a borrower. For through this <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span> who does not desire any <span class="key-term">Olam Haba</span> — who rather has complete <span class="key-term">simchah</span> from the <span class="key-term">mitzvah</span> itself — not only is he not called a borrower and <span class="key-term">m'kabeil</span> at all, but to the contrary: he merits to be called a lender — as though he lends and bestows upon Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span>, in the aspect of: <span class="scripture">"One who is gracious to the poor lends to Hashem"</span> <span class="source">(Proverbs 19:17)</span>. For this <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span> is "one who is gracious to the poor" — he does kindness and graciousness with the entire world that was called poor and destitute until now, in the aspect of a <span class="key-term">m'kabeil</span>. And through this <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span>, the world is rectified, as above. And he is considered as though he lends and bestows upon Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span> — for <span class="scripture">"the Tzadik rules"</span> over Him, <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span>, as our Sages, <em>zichronam livrachah</em>, said: the aspect of <span class="scripture">"a Tzadik who rules in the fear of God"</span> <span class="source">(II Samuel 23:3)</span>. And our Sages, <em>zichronam livrachah</em>, said: "Who rules over Me? The <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span>." For he is called ruler and lender and bestower upon Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span>, <span class="key-term">kiv'yachol</span>.</p>
<p>And therefore regarding <span class="key-term">Shabbos</span> — which is the aspect of the aforementioned <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span>, the aspect of <span class="scripture">"an eternal covenant between Me and the children of Israel"</span> <span class="source">(Exodus 31:17)</span> etc. — it is said: "Borrow on My account, and I will repay." For through <span class="key-term">Shabbos</span>, which is the aspect of the aforementioned <span class="key-term">mitzvah b'simchah</span>, through this the Holy One, Blessed is He, becomes the aspect of a borrower, as above.</p>

<div class="section-header">§17</div>
<p>Therefore, through the <span class="key-term">simchah</span> of <span class="key-term">Shabbos</span>, all the worlds ascend to their root. And <span class="key-term">zachor</span> and <span class="key-term">shamor</span> are included together — the aspect of <span class="scripture">"zachor and shamor were said in a single utterance"</span> — which is the aspect of "Hashem is One and His Name One," as above. This one merits through the aforementioned <span class="key-term">simchah</span>, which is the <span class="key-term">simchah</span> of <span class="key-term">Shabbos</span>, as above. And therefore we say in <span class="key-term">Kabbalas Shabbos</span>: <span class="aramaic">"V'ha ukimna d'Hashem echad ush'mo echad"</span> ["And we have established that Hashem is One and His Name One"].</p>
<p>For the essential completeness is to include both aspects together: the <span class="key-term">aspaklaria ham'irah</span> and the <span class="key-term">aspaklaria she'ainah m'irah</span> — which is the aspect of the inclusion of "after the creation" in "before the creation," which is the aspect of "Hashem is One and His Name One." That is, one must toil and labor until he merits to reach the aforementioned level — to perform the <span class="key-term">mitzvos</span> with <span class="key-term">simchah</span> from the <span class="key-term">mitzvah</span> itself etc., as above. And then the entire world is included in its root, as above. It follows that the totality of the worlds — which are the aspect of <span class="key-term">m'kabeil</span> relative to Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span>, for they were all created by Him and receive their vitality from Him, <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span>, at every moment, and because they are receivers, some aspect of sadness takes hold of them, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">din</span>, as above — but the <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span>, through his complete service with true <span class="key-term">simchah</span>, as above, for which the entire world was created, as above — he elevates the world to its root. For now the world is not in the category of <span class="key-term">m'kabeil</span> from Him, <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span>, at all — for everything is included in the <span class="key-term">Tzadik moshel</span> [ruling Tzadik], who is the aspect of <span class="key-term">mashpia</span>. It follows that the aspect of <span class="key-term">m'kabeil</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">aspaklaria she'ainah m'irah</span> — which is the totality of the worlds — is included in the aspect of <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">mashpia</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">aspaklaria ham'irah</span>. And this is the aspect of: <span class="scripture">"the two great luminaries"</span> <span class="source">(Genesis 1:16)</span>.</p>
<p>And therefore all the prophets — even though their prophecy was through the <span class="key-term">aspaklaria she'ainah m'irah</span>, and therefore no prophet is permitted to innovate a matter, and we are unable to perform any <span class="key-term">mitzvah</span> or practice through them, as above — nevertheless we need their true prophecy. For the entire essence of their prophecy was only to uphold and strengthen and urge and admonish Israel that they should fulfill the Torah of Moshe Rabbeinu, <em>alav hashalom</em>. And as Malachi, who is the last of all the prophets, concluded: <span class="scripture">"Remember the Torah of Moshe"</span> <span class="source">(Malachi 3:22)</span> etc. For this is the essence of all the prophecy of the prophets — to admonish Israel that they should fulfill the Torah of Moshe. And through this, all their prophecy is considered the aspect of <span class="key-term">aspaklaria ham'irah</span> — for they all connect themselves to Moshe, who is the master of all the prophets, and they are all included in him and receive from him. For all the prophets stood at Mount Sinai, as our Sages, <em>zichronam livrachah</em>, said. And the connection and inclusion of all the prophets in Moshe Rabbeinu — this is the aspect of the inclusion of the <span class="key-term">aspaklaria she'ainah m'irah</span> in the <span class="key-term">aspaklaria ham'irah</span>, which is the aspect of the inclusion of the worlds in their root. Through this everything is rectified, as above. And therefore all the prophets and <span class="key-term">Tzadikim</span> rectified the world and completed the world from its deficiencies — and everything was through the power and merit and connection of the truly great <span class="key-term">Tzadikim</span> who are the aspect of Moshe, the aspect of <span class="key-term">aspaklaria ham'irah</span>, as above.</p>

<div class="section-header">§18</div>
<p>And therefore "one who circumcised but did not perform <span class="key-term">pri'ah</span>, it is as though he did not circumcise." For as long as this thin <span class="key-term">k'lipah</span> of the <span class="key-term">pri'ah</span> remains — whose hold is from the second aspect, as above, that one desires to receive <span class="key-term">Olam Haba</span> as his reward — even though he has already cut the <span class="key-term">orlah</span>, it can still take hold, <span class="key-term">chas v'shalom</span>. For the essential hold of the <span class="key-term">orlah</span> and the <span class="key-term">k'lipos</span> is from this aspect — from the aspect of <span class="key-term">m'kabeil</span>, as above. And it is impossible to rectify the world and to subdue the <span class="key-term">k'lipos</span> completely except through this <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span> who is the aspect of Moshe, the aspect of <span class="key-term">aspaklaria ham'irah</span>, who does not desire any <span class="key-term">Olam Haba</span>. Through him specifically all the <span class="key-term">k'lipos</span> are subdued and nullified — which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">pri'ah</span> etc., as above. Through this specifically the <span class="key-term">Bris</span> is revealed — the aspect of <span class="key-term">Tzadik Y'sod Olam</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">mashpia</span>, as above.</p>
<p>And therefore everyone must connect themselves to the truly great <span class="key-term">Tzadikim</span> of the highest level, who are the aspect of Moshe, <span class="key-term">aspaklaria ham'irah</span>. And as our Sages, <em>zichronam livrachah</em>, said: that in every generation there are <span class="key-term">Tzadikim</span> who gaze through the <span class="key-term">aspaklaria ham'irah</span>. For only through such <span class="key-term">Tzadikim</span> is it possible to nullify all the <span class="key-term">k'lipos</span> and to sweeten all the judgments and to complete all the deficiencies and to serve Hashem in truth with true <span class="key-term">simchah</span>. <em>Amein v'amein.</em></p>

<div class="section-header">§19</div>
<p>And this is what our Rebbe, <em>zichrono livrachah</em>, wrote in Sefer Ha'Alef-Beis HeChadash <span class="source">(entry: <em>Mohel</em>, Siman 6)</span>: that it is good to do <span class="key-term">chesed</span> [kindness] before giving the name to the infant, in order that the name should endure etc. — see there. For one must then do <span class="key-term">chesed</span> in order to be in the aspect of a <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span> who performs <span class="key-term">tzedakah</span> and <span class="key-term">chesed</span> — through which a person is called a <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span>, the aspect of "<span class="key-term">Tzadik</span> who is gracious and gives," as above, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">mashpia</span>. In order that the <span class="key-term">Shaim</span>, the aspect of the totality of the worlds, should be included in <span class="aramaic">sitra did'chura</span>, in the aspect of <span class="key-term">chesed</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">mashpia</span> — the opposite of the aspect of <span class="key-term">m'kabeil</span>, where the <span class="key-term">din</span> takes hold, <span class="key-term">chas v'shalom</span>.</p>
<p>For the essential thing is to sweeten the <span class="key-term">Shaim</span> — which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">malchus</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">m'kabeil</span>, as is known, for <span class="aramaic">lais leih migarmeih klum</span> ["it has nothing of its own"] — to sweeten it so that it should be in the aspect of <span class="key-term">chesed</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">mashpia</span>. That the light of the moon should be as the light of the sun — which is the aspect of remembering the <span class="key-term">Shaim</span>, the aspect of "Hashem is One and His Name One." For in truth all is one, as above.</p>

<div class="section-header">§20</div>
<p>And this is what is written in Jeremiah <span class="source">(31:21–22)</span>, in the consolations that he prophesied about the days of <span class="key-term">Mashiach</span> — may he come speedily in our days: <span class="scripture">"How long will you turn away, O wayward daughter? For Hashem has created a new thing in the earth — a woman shall encircle a man."</span> And Rashi explained that the <span class="key-term">n'kaivah</span> [female] shall encircle to become a <span class="key-term">gever</span> [man] — that is, <span class="key-term">K'nesses Yisroel</span> [the Congregation of Israel] shall be in the aspect of a <span class="key-term">gever</span> who inherits everything etc. — see there.</p>
<p>That is, as above: the essential source of all the troubles and decrees that Israel has suffered throughout all the days of the exile — everything is drawn from the root of the <span class="key-term">din</span> at the beginning of creation, from the aspect of sadness, the aspect of "and He was saddened to His heart" — which is drawn from the fact that the world is the aspect of a <span class="key-term">m'kabeil</span> when it does not merit to fulfill the will of the Place in completeness, with <span class="key-term">simchah</span> from the <span class="key-term">mitzvah</span> itself, as above.</p>
<p>But in the future, in the days of <span class="key-term">Mashiach</span>, the <span class="key-term">simchah</span> will become exceedingly great, as is written in all the verses that speak of the final Redemption, which elaborate greatly on the immensity of the great <span class="key-term">simchah</span> that will be then — as it is written: <span class="scripture">"And I will transform their mourning into joy"</span> <span class="source">(Jeremiah 31:12)</span> etc.; <span class="scripture">"joy and simchah shall overtake, and sorrow and sighing shall flee"</span> <span class="source">(Isaiah 35:10)</span>; <span class="scripture">"for with simchah you shall go forth"</span> <span class="source">(Isaiah 55:12)</span> etc. And many more like these. That is, we will then merit the aforementioned <span class="key-term">simchah</span> — to rejoice in Him, <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span>, as it is written: <span class="scripture">"We will rejoice and be glad in You"</span> <span class="source">(Shir HaShirim 1:4)</span> — the aspect of <span class="scripture">"Israel shall rejoice in its Maker"</span>. That is, that we will exult and rejoice from the <span class="key-term">mitzvah</span> itself, which is His unity, <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span> — which is the ultimate <span class="key-term">simchah</span>, as above. And through this the entire world will be rectified — for it will leave the aspect of <span class="key-term">m'kabeil</span> and enter the aspect of <span class="key-term">mashpia</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">Tzadik moshel</span>, as above.</p>
<p>And this is: <span class="scripture">"For Hashem has created a new thing in the earth — a woman shall encircle a man."</span> That is, <span class="key-term">K'nesses Yisroel</span> — which is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">Shaim</span> of Hashem, in which all the worlds are contained from beginning to end — which was until now the aspect of <span class="key-term">n'kaivah</span> [female], the aspect of <span class="key-term">m'kabeil</span>, from which came the hold of all the judgments etc., as above — now "the <span class="key-term">n'kaivah</span> shall encircle to become a <span class="key-term">gever</span>" — the aspect of <span class="aramaic">sitra did'chura</span>, which is <span class="key-term">mashpia</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">Tzadik moshel</span>. Through the immensity of the <span class="key-term">simchah</span> in His <span class="key-term">mitzvos</span>, as above — through this everything is sweetened and rectified, as above.</p>

<div class="section-header">§21</div>
<p>And this is what is written in the writings of the Arizal: that in the future the verse <span class="scripture">"a woman of valor is the crown of her husband"</span> <span class="source">(Proverbs 12:4)</span> will be fulfilled. That is, as above: <span class="key-term">malchus</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">Shaim</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">K'nesses Yisroel</span>, the aspect of the totality of the worlds — will be, <span class="key-term">kiv'yachol</span>, above, the aspect of <span class="key-term">mashpia</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">Tzadik moshel</span>, the aspect of <span class="aramaic">islab'shas b'manay dich'ura</span> ["she garbs herself in masculine garments"] etc., <span class="key-term">kiv'yachol</span>. As though Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span> receives from us — through our performing our <span class="key-term">mitzvos</span> with <span class="key-term">simchah</span>, not desiring any <span class="key-term">Olam Haba</span>. And this is the aspect of: <span class="scripture">"Who is a chasid? One who does kindness with his Creator"</span> — as above. [This pertains to the above.]</p>

<div class="section-header">§22</div>
<p>And this is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">mitzvah</span> of lending grace and performing kindness, as it is written: <span class="scripture">"If you lend money to My people"</span> <span class="source">(Exodus 22:24)</span> — and our Sages, <em>zichronam livrachah</em>, said that this is an obligation.</p>
<p>For we must cleave to His attributes. And just as Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span> sustained His world with kindness, as a loan, from the day of the creation until <span class="key-term">Matan Torah</span> — that all the worlds were sustained only as a loan and kindness, as above — and also after <span class="key-term">Matan Torah</span>, every time they transgress His will, <span class="key-term">chas v'shalom</span>, it would be fitting for the world to revert to chaos and void, for the world has no existence without the Torah — and then the world, and especially the sinning person, is sustained only through His kindness, in the aspect of a loan: that Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span> extends time for him until he returns and repays and discharges to Him what he has come to owe — many debts through his sins. And this is the aspect of <span class="key-term">erech apayim</span> [slow to anger], the aspect of <span class="aramaic">archa isyahiv leih</span> ["he is given length"] — which is the aspect of the extension of time, that Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span> bestows kindness upon the person and waits and extends time for him, time after time, many many times, until he returns. And then he discharges everything. For through this, <span class="key-term">t'shuvah</span> is called <span class="aramaic">"zarka"</span> — on account of <span class="aramaic">d'izd'rikas la'asar d'isn'tilat mitaman</span> — "that it is cast back to the place from which it was taken." He returns and restores the thing to the place from which it was taken — as our Rebbe, <em>zichrono livrachah</em>, wrote in another place <span class="source">(Siman 35)</span>.</p>
<p>It follows that Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span> fulfills the <span class="key-term">mitzvah</span> of performing kindness and lending grace very greatly, at every moment. So too, we are obligated to cleave to His attributes and to have mercy on the poor and to lend to them, and not to be as a creditor to him — as it is written in the aforementioned verse — just as Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span> does not press us, and extends for us until we discharge little by little, until we merit to discharge everything. <em>Amein.</em></p>

<div class="section-header">§23</div>
<p>And this is the aspect of: <span class="scripture">"The mother of the children rejoices, Halleluyah!"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 113:9)</span>. <span class="scripture">"When Israel went out from Egypt… Judah became His sanctuary, Israel His dominion"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 114:1–2)</span>. For through the <span class="key-term">simchah</span> of <span class="key-term">K'nesses Yisroel</span> — which is called "the mother of the children" — that they had at the time of the Exodus from Egypt and the receiving of the Torah, when the <span class="key-term">simchah</span> was exceedingly great — as our Sages, <em>zichronam livrachah</em>, said <span class="source">(Shabbos 88a)</span>: <span class="scripture">"And eternal simchah upon their heads"</span> — which is said about the crowns of "na'aseh v'nishma" [we will do and we will hear] that they said at the time of receiving the Torah — through this <span class="key-term">simchah</span> they merited the aspect of <span class="key-term">Tzadik moshel</span>, the aspect of <span class="scripture">"Israel His dominion."</span> For through the greatness of the <span class="key-term">simchah</span>, one merits the aspect of <span class="key-term">Tzadik moshel</span> — the aspect of "Israel His dominion" — that Israel rules over Him, <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span>, <span class="key-term">kiv'yachol</span>. For He decrees a decree, and they nullify it — as our Sages, <em>zichronam livrachah</em>, said. And everything is through the <span class="key-term">simchas hamitzvah</span>, as above.</p>
<p>And this is how it concludes: <span class="scripture">"The sea saw and fled"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 114:3)</span> etc. That is, to merit the aforementioned <span class="key-term">simchas hamitzvah</span> — which is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">simchah</span> of receiving the Torah — this one merits through the aspect of the splitting of the Sea of Reeds, which is the aspect of prayer with power, which is the aspect of "thunder" — the aspect of: <span class="scripture">"Let the sea thunder and all that fills it"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 96:11)</span>. As our Rebbe, <em>zichrono livrachah</em>, wrote there in the aforementioned teaching, explaining it well — as it is written regarding the verse: <span class="scripture">"And the sea returned toward morning to its power"</span> <span class="source">(Exodus 14:27)</span> — which is the aspect of the aforementioned "thunder." Through this one merits the aforementioned <span class="key-term">simchah</span>. Through this one merits the aspect of <span class="key-term">Tzadik moshel</span> — <span class="scripture">"Israel His dominion"</span> — as above.</p>

<div class="translator-summary">
<h3>Summary of Ha'oseh Shaliach LGC 2, Part 2 (§§13–23)</h3>
<p>§13: Milah and pri'ah. Or ha'orlah = k'lipah g'murah (ra gamur) → entices away from Hashem. Or hapri'ah = k'lipas nogah (thin k'lipah) → from one who still wants s'char Olam Haba = slightly m'kabeil → slight atzvus → thin k'lipah. Must tear both to reveal Tzadik Y'sod Olam = mashpia. Pri'ah = lashon pri'ah v'shilum (pri'as ba'al chov) → only through Tzadik who does mitzvah b'simchah from mitzvah itself → world returns to shoresh → pays chov hab'riah.</p>
<p>§14: Kos yayin after milah = yayin ham'samei'ach → simchah of milah. Simchah shel asid = yayin ham'shumar l'asid ("nagilah v'nism'chah bach nazkiiah dodecha miyayin," Shir HaShirim 1:4).</p>
<p>§15: Shaim given after milah (= simchah). Man created to include "achar hab'riah" in "kodem hab'riah" → nullify atzvus/din ("vayis'atzeiv el libo") → "yismach Hashem b'ma'asav." Only through simchah from mitzvah itself (= achduso) → ain atzvus lifnay HaMakom → ain lavo el sha'ar HaMelech bilvush sak. K'laliyus ha'olam = Shaim Hashem (revelation of malchuso). When world niklal in Hashem through simchah → "Hashem echad ush'mo echad" (Zechariah 14:9) = achar hab'riah niklal b'kodem. Rabbeinu zl (Sichos HaRa"N): through atzvus → doesn't know sh'mo → forgets shaim after death. Created to include zachor v'shamor yachad (aspaklaria she'ainah m'irah b'aspaklaria ham'irah = Hashem echad ush'mo echad). Shamor = m'kabeil/atzvus → must merit through simchah → nullify m'kabeil → or hal'vanah k'or hachamah → "es sh'nay ham'oros hag'dolim." Tzadik through ma'asim → worlds not m'kabeilim → niklal b'Tzadik mashpia → zachor + shamor = Hashem echad ush'mo echad. Atzvus → shochai'ach sh'mo → blemishes zikaron (sitra did'chura). After milah (= simchah) → shaim given → Shaim niklal b'shorsho.</p>
<p>§16: Shabbos: "l'vu alai va'ani porai'a." Shabbos = mitzvah b'simchah from mitzvah itself (Olam Haba in mitzvah itself = Shabbos = mai'ain Olam Haba = yom simchah v'chedvah). "Zachor es yom haShabbos: zachraihu mai'echad b'Shabbos." On Shabbos all worlds ascend to shoresh (through simchas hamitzvah) → siluk chovos → world exits m'kabeil. "L'vu alai va'ani porai'a": through Shabbos, HKB"H becomes "loveh" kiv'yachol → Tzadik not m'kabeil at all → l'heipech: called Malveh → "malveh Hashem chonaim dal" (Mishlei 19:17) = chonaim dal (world that was ani v'dal as m'kabeil). Tzadik moshel: "mi moshel bi? Tzadik." Shabbos = Bris olam → "l'vu alai va'ani porai'a."</p>
<p>§17: Simchas Shabbos → worlds ascend → zachor v'shamor b'dibur echad = Hashem echad ush'mo echad (said in Kabbalas Shabbos). Sh'laimus: include aspaklaria ham'irah + she'ainah m'irah = achar hab'riah b'kodem = Hashem echad ush'mo echad. Worlds = m'kabeil (receive chiyus from Hashem) → atzvus/din. Tzadik → simchah → world ascends → not m'kabeil → niklal in Tzadik moshel (mashpia) → aspaklaria she'ainah m'irah niklal in ham'irah = "sh'nay ham'oros hag'dolim." All n'vi'im: aspaklaria she'ainah m'irah → ain rashai l'chadeish davar → but we need their n'vuah (to uphold/strengthen Torah of Moshe, as Malachi concluded "zichru Toras Moshe"). Their n'vuah thus = aspaklaria ham'irah (all connected to Moshe, raban shel kol han'vi'im; all stood at Sinai). Inclusion of n'vi'im in Moshe = aspaklaria she'ainah m'irah in ham'irah = worlds in root → tikkun. All n'vi'im/Tzadikim rectified world through ko'ach/z'chus/hiskashrus to Tzadikim ha'amitiyim hag'dolim = b'chinas Moshe.</p>
<p>§18: Mal v'lo para k'ilu lo mal → thin k'lipah of pri'ah (m'kabeil/wants Olam Haba) can reattach even after milah. Only Tzadik (Moshe/aspaklaria ham'irah, doesn't want Olam Haba) → subdues all k'lipos = pri'ah → reveals Bris (Tzadik Y'sod Olam, mashpia). Must connect to Tzadikim ha'amitiyim hag'dolim (b'chinas Moshe aspaklaria ham'irah). In every dor: Tzadikim who gaze through aspaklaria ham'irah → through them alone → nullify k'lipos, sweeten dinim, complete chesronos, serve Hashem b'simchah amitis. Amein v'amein.</p>
<p>§19: Rabbeinu zl (Sefer Ha'AB HeChadash, Mohel 6): do chesed before naming → shaim endures. Chesed → b'chinas Tzadik (tzedakah v'chesed = chonaim v'no'sain = mashpia) → Shaim (k'laliyus worlds) included in sitra did'chura/chesed/mashpia (heipech m'kabeil where din takes hold). Sweeten Shaim (= malchus/m'kabeil, lais leih migarmeih klum) → chesed/mashpia → or hal'vanah k'or hachamah → zichras haShaim → Hashem echad ush'mo echad.</p>
<p>§20: Yirmiyah 31:21-22 (consolations for y'mos haMashiach): "Ad masai tischamkin habas hashovavah ki vara Hashem chadashah ba'aretz n'kaivah t'soveiv gaver." Rashi: n'kaivah becomes gever (K'nesses Yisroel = gever who inherits). All tzaros/g'zairos of galus = root din of creation (atzvus/m'kabeil). In future → simchah exceedingly great ("v'hafachti evlam l'sason"; "sason v'simchah yasigu v'nasu yagon va'anachah"; "ki b'simchah taitzai'u"). Merit simchah in Him ("nagilah v'nism'chah bach" = yismach Yisroel b'osav = simchah from mitzvah itself = achduso = tachlis hasimchah → tikkun kol ha'olam). "N'kaivah t'soveiv gever": K'nesses Yisroel (= Shaim, k'laliyus worlds) was n'kaivah/m'kabeil → now becomes gever (sitra did'chura = mashpia = Tzadik moshel) through otzem hasimchah.</p>
<p>§21: Arizal: l'asid → "aishes chayil ateres ba'alah" (Mishlei 12:4). Malchus/Shaim/K'nesses Yisroel/k'laliyus worlds → l'maalah (mashpia/Tzadik moshel) → islab'shas b'manay dich'ura → as if Hashem m'kabeil from us (mitzvos b'simchah, no Olam Haba) = "aizehu chasid hamischaseid im kono."</p>
<p>§22: Mitzvah of halvaas chain ug'milus chesed: "Im kesef talveh es ami" (Sh'mos 22:24, Chazal: chovah). Must cleave to midos of Hashem. Hashem sustained world b'chesed as halvaah (from creation to Matan Torah). Also after Matan Torah, when transgress → world sustained b'chesed as halvaah → Hashem extends time = erech apayim = archa isyahiv leih → until returns and pays. T'shuvah = "zarka" (Rabbeinu zl Siman 35) = izd'rikas la'asar d'isn'tilat (returns thing to its place). Hashem fulfills g'milus chesed constantly → we must also → lend to poor, not be as nosheh → as Hashem is not nosheh → extends until we pay little by little. Amein.</p>
<p>§23: "Aim habanim s'maichah Halleluyah" (T'hilim 113:9); "B'tzais Yisroel miMitzrayim...haysah Y'hudah l'kodsho Yisroel mamsh'losav" (T'hilim 114:1-2). Through simchah of K'nesses Yisroel (= aim habanim) at Y'tzias Mitzrayim and Kabbalas HaTorah (great simchah: "v'simchas olam al rosham," Shabbos 88a = kisray na'aseh v'nishma) → merited Tzadik moshel = "Yisroel mamsh'losav" (Israel rules over Him kiv'yachol: He decrees, they nullify). All through simchas hamitzvah. "Hayam ra'ah vayanos" (T'hilim 114:3) → simchas hamitzvah (= simchas Kabbalas HaTorah) merited through b'chinas kri'as Yam Suf = t'filah b'ko'ach = r'amim ("yir'am hayam um'lo'o") → Rabbeinu zl on "vayashav hayam lifnos boker l'aisano" = r'amim → simchah → Tzadik moshel = Yisroel mamsh'losav.</p>
<p class="translator-note">This completes Laws of One Who Appoints an Agent to Collect His Debt, Halachah 2. Based on Likutay Moharan I:5 "With Trumpets and the Sound of the Shofar."</p>
